<p class="meta">Range of View Studios · CTRL-A · Internal Strategy · July 8, 2026</p>
<h1>The CTRL-A Gating Architecture</h1>
<p class="subtitle">How we gate valuable things behind at least an email, and eventually payment, without rebuilding anything twice.</p>

<h2>Part 1 · Audit: What We Have Today</h2>
<p>The value ladder is half built, and the gates are decorative in two places. The strategic gap in one sentence: <b>we have identity collection, but no enforcement layer.</b> The site never proves someone is a lead, member, or customer before handing over value. It just politely asks.</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Status</th><th>Honest weakness</th></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="gold">Klaviyo email capture</span></td><td>Live in production</td><td>None. This part is solid. Three forms feed list Syw3GS, each tagged with its source.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>ToolGate (email unlock)</td><td>Built, uncommitted</td><td><b>A curtain, not a lock.</b> The gated files live in the public folder, so anyone with the URL downloads them free. The unlocked state is a localStorage flag anyone can set in devtools.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="violet">Supabase accounts + credits</span></td><td>Wallet live; earn engine built, dormant</td><td>Spending is only wired to brand-kit exports. Credits cannot buy downloads or course content yet.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="rose">Payments</span></td><td>Does not exist</td><td>Nothing to audit. Greenfield.</td></tr>
</table>

<p>That is fine for markdown prompt packs, where the cost of leakage is zero and the gate is a conversion device. It is <b>not</b> fine the moment we gate anything worth paying for. The architecture below fixes enforcement once, in a way that scales from "email required" to "payment required" without a rebuild.</p>

<h2>Part 2 · The Architecture: One Entitlement Ladder</h2>
<p>Every visitor holds one of four keys, each strictly stronger than the last. Every valuable thing on the site declares the minimum key it requires. One rule, applied everywhere.</p>

<div class="keyline">
KEY 0 &nbsp; Anonymous &nbsp;&nbsp; nothing proven<br>
KEY 1 &nbsp; Lead &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; proved an email &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &rarr; issued by Klaviyo subscribe<br>
KEY 2 &nbsp; Member &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; proved an identity &nbsp; &rarr; issued by Supabase login<br>
KEY 3 &nbsp; Customer &nbsp;&nbsp; proved a payment &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &rarr; issued by Stripe webhook
</div>

<h3>Piece 1 · Make the Lead key provable (the critical upgrade)</h3>
<p>Today the "I gave my email" state is a spoofable localStorage flag. The upgrade: when the Klaviyo subscribe succeeds, the <b>server</b> sets a signed, HttpOnly cookie, a "lead token" (an HMAC of the email with a server secret). The browser cannot forge it because it does not know the secret. Costs the user nothing extra: same form, same single field. But now the server can verify leadship.</p>

<h3>Piece 2 · Move gated assets behind a delivery route</h3>
<p>Files leave the public folder and move into a private one. A single new route serves them:</p>
<div class="keyline">
GET /api/vault/[file]<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&rarr; has valid lead cookie? &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; serve the file<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&rarr; OR valid Supabase session? &nbsp;&nbsp; serve the file<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&rarr; OR paid entitlement? &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; serve the file<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&rarr; else &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 401, "unlock with your email"
</div>
<p>Now the gate is real: no URL sharing bypass, no devtools bypass. The same route pattern later serves paid PDFs, video links, anything. We never rebuild it.</p>

<h3>Piece 3 · An entitlements table in Supabase (the payment-ready core)</h3>
<p>One small table: <b>user_id / entitlement / granted_by / expires_at</b>. Rows get created three ways:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Credits:</b> spending 100 credits on a course chapter inserts <i>premium-course</i>, granted_by <i>credits</i>. The earn engine already built becomes a real economy.</li>
  <li><b>Stripe:</b> when payments arrive, a Stripe webhook inserts the same row, granted_by <i>stripe</i>. Checkout is hosted by Stripe; we never touch cards, no PCI burden.</li>
  <li><b>Manual:</b> we comp a client directly from the Supabase dashboard.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><b>The beauty:</b> the delivery route does not care how an entitlement was earned. Credits, cash, or comp: same check. That is what makes this scale instead of becoming spaghetti.</div>

<h3>How the tiers map to actual CTRL-A content</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Content</th><th>Key required</th><th>Why</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Prompt Mixer, The Fold, course reading</td><td>0 · Open</td><td>The hook. Never gate the demo.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Prompt pack, cheatsheet, templates</td><td>1 · Lead</td><td>Lead magnets. Email is the price.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Earning and spending credits, saved work</td><td>2 · Member</td><td>Needs identity to hold a balance.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Premium packs, advanced chapters, brand-kit top-ups</td><td>3 · Customer <i>or</i> credits</td><td>Two currencies for the same door: engagement (credits) or money (Stripe). Engaged non-payers still deepen; impatient payers just pay.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Part 3 · Phased Rollout (each phase ships alone)</h2>
<ol>
  <li><b>Phase 1 · Real email gate.</b> Lead cookie + /api/vault delivery route + move files out of public. Small job, roughly two to three files, uses only what is already deployed. <b>This is the "at least an email" enforcement.</b></li>
  <li><b>Phase 2 · Activate credits.</b> Run the SQL migration in Supabase, add the service-role key to Vercel, wire spend_credits to premium downloads and chapters. Everything is built; this is switch flipping plus one spend route.</li>
  <li><b>Phase 3 · Stripe.</b> Checkout for credit packs and direct unlocks; one webhook writing entitlements. Only after Phase 1 and 2 data shows people actually hitting the paid wall.</li>
</ol>

<div class="warn"><b>Strategic warning:</b> do not ship Phase 3 first or simultaneously. Payments before we have measured demand at the email gate is building a tollbooth before the road has traffic. Phase 1's data (unlock rates per gate, via the ctrla_source tags already flowing into Klaviyo) tells us what is worth charging for.</div>

<h2>Appendix · Who Does What (the plain-English map)</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th></th><th class="gold">Klaviyo</th><th>Supabase</th><th>Stripe (future)</th></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Role</b></td><td>The megaphone. Talks to people by email.</td><td>The membership desk. Remembers who people are.</td><td>The cash register. Proves payment.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><b>Holds</b></td><td>Email list, welcome flow, drops</td><td>Accounts, credits, entitlements</td><td>Cards, checkout, receipts</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Friction</b></td><td>Just an email</td><td>Sign in with Google</td><td>A purchase</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><b>Status</b></td><td>Live</td><td>Wallet live, earn engine dormant</td><td>Not started</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A Klaviyo signup does <b>not</b> create a Supabase account. The bridge runs one way only: every new Supabase member is also copied onto the Klaviyo list, so the email list is always the widest pool and members are the committed core inside it.</p>

<p class="footer">CTRL-A Gating Architecture · Range of View Studios · Prepared July 8, 2026 · Companion visual: the CTRL-A System Map artifact</p>

